# Apache rules shipped with the built site.
#
# This file is copied into dist/ on every build, and the deploy publishes dist/
# into releases/<timestamp>/ on the host, with a `current` symlink flipped at
# the newest one. So these rules land inside every release and apply whether a
# request arrives via the symlink or a release directory directly.
#
# It deliberately lives here rather than in the docroot .htaccess one level
# above releases/, which is hand-maintained on the host and not deployed by CI.
# Rules do work there, so anything this repo should be able to change on its own
# belongs in this file instead.
#
# One thing cannot live here: folding www and http into https://trussphp.com.
# That has to run before the docroot's own rewrite into current/, so the docroot
# file owns it. A tracked, reference-only copy is in deploy/docroot.htaccess,
# which explains the ordering trap that kept a redirect dead there for days.

RewriteEngine On

# The deploy plumbing was reachable from the web: /current/ served the entire
# site a second time, which split analytics by page path and offered search
# engines a duplicate of every page. Redirect it to the canonical URL instead.
#
# Matching %{THE_REQUEST} (the client's original request line) rather than the
# rewritten path is what makes this safe: any internal rewrite into current/
# performed at the docroot leaves THE_REQUEST untouched, so ordinary page views
# are unaffected and only requests that genuinely target current/ or
# releases/<ts>/ are caught.
#
# ^(.*)$ keeps the remainder of the path, because per-directory rewriting has
# already stripped the matched prefix: /current/guides/theming/ redirects to
# /guides/theming/ rather than collapsing to the home page.
#
# %{HTTP_HOST} rather than a hardcoded domain, so a preview or staging host
# redirects to itself instead of bouncing traffic to production.
RewriteCond %{THE_REQUEST} \s/+(current|releases)(/|\?|\s) [NC]
RewriteRule ^(.*)$ https://%{HTTP_HOST}/$1 [R=301,L]

# One page, one URL. /demo/index.html and /demo/ served identical bytes, and
# Search Console listed both. The canonical tag already resolved which one wins,
# so this is tidiness rather than a fix: it saves a crawler the fetch it would
# otherwise spend being told the same thing.
#
# %{THE_REQUEST} again, for a sharper reason than above. /demo/ is served by
# internally resolving index.html, so matching the rewritten path would redirect
# /demo/ to /demo/ forever. The request line only says index.html when the
# client actually asked for it.
#
# ^(.*/)?index\.html$ makes the directory optional, so /index.html folds to /
# as well. Per-directory rewriting has already stripped the prefix, so $1 is the
# directory below the site root.
RewriteCond %{THE_REQUEST} \s/+[^?\s]*index\.html[\s?] [NC]
RewriteRule ^(.*/)?index\.html$ https://%{HTTP_HOST}/$1 [R=301,L]

# ErrorDocument resolves its local path against the DOCROOT, not against the
# directory this file sits in. The docroot has no 404.html of its own, since the
# built page only exists inside each release, so a plain `/404.html` silently
# fails and Apache serves its own generic error page. Point it through the
# symlink instead.
ErrorDocument 404 /current/404.html
